From 22039b07d57301b14631c650b3de070fc2654344 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Pelenitsyn <a.pelenitsyn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:04:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Rewrite packages for mhs as MicroCabal does, before
 solving

MicroCabal rewrites the dependencies of every package it builds with mhs:
it adds ghc-compat, which holds modules that are part of base in GHC but
not in mhs, such as GHC.Stack and Language.Haskell.TH.Syntax, and it uses
the forks array-mhs and random-mhs instead of array and random, which mhs
cannot compile. Packages written for GHC rely on both.

Distribution.Simple.MHS.rewritePackageDescription does the same, and
Distribution.Simple.Configure.packageDescriptionForCompiler dispatches to it
for mhs. cabal-install applies it to all the packages it gives to the solver
(rewriteSourcePackages, next to the rewriting for allow-newer), so that the
solver chooses ghc-compat and the forks, and cabal-install builds them from
Hackage like any other dependency; configure applies it to the package it
configures, as far as cabal-install passes it those packages. Nothing has to
be installed for mhs beforehand, and offline, where they cannot be had, the
packages are left as they are. See Note [MicroHs rewrites of packages].

As with MicroCabal, the version macros of a fork are also defined under the
name of the package it forks: QuickCheck, which depends on random, tests
MIN_VERSION_random(1,3,0) to define its RandomGen instance.

